<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William C. Gorgas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Crawford Gorgas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William C. Gorgas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army physician who, as chief sanitary officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> from 1904, brought yellow fever and malaria under control by destroying the mosquitoes that carried them. Applying methods he had used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-reed">Walter Reed</a>'s work on yellow fever transmission, he made the American construction of the canal possible. He later served as Surgeon General of the United States Army from 1914 to 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-c-gorgas.jpg" alt="William C. Gorgas" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing, photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GORGAS,_W.C._GENERAL_LCCN2016857062_Trim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States built the canal between 1904 and 1914, after <b>William Gorgas</b>’s sanitation campaign brought yellow fever and malaria under control, employing tens of thousands of workers, many of them from Barbados, Jamaica and other Caribbean islands, whose descendants form an important Afro-Caribbean, English-speaking community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>American construction began in 1904; <b>William Gorgas</b>'s campaign against mosquitoes conquered yellow fever, and George Washington Goethals completed the canal, which opened in August 1914.</blockquote><a href="/state/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mobile, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.73,-88.05&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Mobile on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2270 1043 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William C. Gorgas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2720 1313h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2720 1313h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.73,-88.05&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
